    description: "Surfaced by the WI-falum degenerate-fingerprint cross_field check on the\n2026-06-18 full-corpus self-analysis (35281 symbols, selfh/dev 288a5a0190).\nDISTINCT from WI-falum (done): that was TOML dependency nodes, hgfp1: scheme.\nThis is the hgfp2: scheme on Python test-stub functions.\n\nObserved: 2 fingerprint values each shared by >=10 distinctly-named symbols —\n  hgfp2:91e04b3962f12994 (23 symbols, 18 names)\n  hgfp2:97672410d1aaf58d (21 symbols, 17 names)\nAll 44 are tiny (2-line) nested stub functions inside test functions:\n  21 in tests/test_analyzer_registry.py, 20 in tests/test_linker_registry.py,\n  3 in tests/test_orchestrator_fail_open.py.\nExamples: test_basic_registration.analyze_rust, test_all_metadata_fields.analyze_js,\ntest_check_requirements_all_met.link_check, test_clears_all.analyze_a.\n\nOPEN QUESTION (bug vs legitimate):\n(a) BUG — the fingerprint post-pass (fingerprint.py stamp_symbol_fingerprints ->\n    _tree_sitter_fingerprint) drops content on these tiny spans and emits a shared\n    constant, the same failure shape as WI-falum's TOML case. If so: unparseable/\n    degenerate spans must yield None, never a shared constant.\n(b) LEGITIMATE — these test stubs really ARE structurally identical (same 2-line\n    body shape, same literals), so a purely-structural fingerprint correctly\n    collides; the check over-fires by treating distinct NAMES as distinct CONTENT.\nResolve by reading the actual stub bodies + how _tree_sitter_fingerprint handles\n2-line function spans. If (a), it is a fingerprint-honesty bug (INV-zadum family);\nif (b), the WI-falum check needs a same-structure carve-out.\n\nScope: 44/35281 (0.12%), warning severity, TEST FILES ONLY (no production source).\nNot a Wave-2-gate blocker (the identity umbrellas closed on stable_id evidence);\ntracked here as a fingerprint-quality residual."  # e36c
    fields: {}  # e36c
